      <header class="ec-essay-header">
        <p class="ec-eyebrow">Philosophy · AI Literacy</p>
        <h1>AI Literacy as Developing Practice</h1>
        <p class="ec-subtitle">Understanding and Building Discipline for Working with AI</p>
        <div class="ec-meta">
          <span class="ec-status ec-status-approved">AI-drafted, Human-Approved</span>
          <time datetime="2026-09-23">2026-09-23</time>
          <span>~15 min read</span>
        </div>
      </header>

      <section class="ec-section">
        <h2>Position</h2>
        <p>AI literacy is a <strong>practice that develops through reflection</strong>, not a checklist to complete. It means understanding enough about AI systems to use them responsibly, evaluate their outputs critically, and maintain human authority over how they are employed.</p>
        <p>AI literacy develops through use—by noticing what we trust, what we change, what we learn, and what we'd do differently. This card offers a framework combining specific rules for consequential decisions with reflective practice for everyday use, because most people won't verify every claim but everyone deserves guidance on when verification matters most.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="ec-section">
        <h2>Why This Matters</h2>
        <p>AI is increasingly embedded in decisions about work, education, health, law, and daily life. Research shows that <strong>over-reliance on AI systems causes real harm</strong>, including documented cases where professionals accepted incorrect AI suggestions without proper verification, sometimes with fatal consequences <cite>(RMIT University, 2026)</cite>.</p>
        <p>The problem isn't that AI is dangerous—it's that <strong>trust calibration is difficult and often fails</strong>. Studies in Nature show that over-trusting automated systems represents a form of automation bias, a cognitive phenomenon where people accept AI outputs without adequate scrutiny <cite>(Nature, 2026)</cite>.</p>
        <p>AI literacy matters because it preserves human agency in a world where AI is becoming more influential and where the "human-in-the-loop" is often more myth than reality—systems reduce cognitive load but also encourage overreliance and inappropriate offloading <cite>(Perry World House, 2025)</cite>.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="ec-section">
        <h2>Substantive Claims</h2>
        <ol class="ec-claims-list">
          <li>
            <strong>AI systems simulate reasoning but do not possess equivalent human cognition.</strong> AI outputs that appear to be reasoning are sophisticated pattern matching and probability-based generation. Understanding this distinction is foundational to critical evaluation. <cite>(Position informed by OECD 2026; Wikipedia "AI literacy")</cite>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>AI literacy requires different practices for different stakes.</strong> High-consequence decisions (medical, legal, financial, safety-critical) require rule-based verification. Routine use benefits from reflective practice that builds discipline over time. <cite>(EU AI Act Article 4; Nature 2026)</cite>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Trust calibration is a learnable skill, not innate.</strong> Research demonstrates that humans can improve their ability to know when to trust AI outputs through deliberate practice and reflection, though transparency and explainability alone do not eliminate the challenge. <cite>(Springer 2026; ResearchGate 2025)</cite>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Reflection after AI interactions improves subsequent judgment.</strong> The metacognitive practice of reviewing what you trusted, what you changed, and what you learned builds better decision-making over time. <cite>(Notre Dame Learning 2021; BERA Journal 2025)</cite>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The user is the sole custodian of how AI is employed.</strong> AI should never control the user; the user must always drive and direct the AI. <cite>(Position informed by EU AI Act Article 4)</cite>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Critical thinking interventions can improve AI reliance behavior.</strong> Empirical studies show that structured critical thinking training improves how people evaluate AI outputs. <cite>(ScienceDirect 2026)</cite>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="ec-section ec-callout-block">
        <h2>Rules for High-Stakes Decisions</h2>
        <p>When AI is used for medical advice, legal interpretation, financial decisions, safety-critical operations, or any decision affecting others' wellbeing:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Verify with independent sources</strong> before acting—do not rely solely on AI's output</li>
          <li><strong>Consult a domain expert</strong> before final action in high-consequence domains</li>
          <li><strong>Cross-reference</strong> the claim with at least one non-AI source</li>
        </ol>
        <p>These rules are not aspirational. Research documents that failing to apply them causes real harm.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="ec-section">
        <h2>Reflection Practice for Everyday Use</h2>
        <p>After each significant AI interaction, ask yourself:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>What did I take from this interaction that I trust?</strong> For each item: Did I verify it? Should I have?</li>
          <li><strong>What did I change or reject?</strong> Why? Was the AI output actually wrong, or just didn't fit my need?</li>
          <li><strong>What did I learn about AI's strengths and limits in this case?</strong> Generalize: Is AI good at this type of task? Less good at another?</li>
          <li><strong>What would I do differently next time?</strong> One small change, not a complete overhaul</li>
        </ol>
        <p>This practice compounds over time. It is not a test you pass or fail—it's a discipline you build.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="ec-section ec-counterargument">
        <h2>Strongest Counterargument</h2>
        <p>The strongest objection is that <strong>this approach is too soft to be meaningful</strong>. Critics argue that everyone already trusts AI too much, that AI literacy should be a concrete skill set with measurable outcomes, and that reflection-based practices are just performative if people won't actually verify AI outputs.</p>
        <p>These objections have merit. This card does not claim that reflection alone solves AI literacy. It claims that <strong>reflection is one pathway among others</strong>, and that for people willing to engage seriously, it builds discipline that rules cannot enforce on their own.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="ec-section">
        <h2>What Would Change My Mind</h2>
        <p>This position would change if evidence showed that:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Prescriptive frameworks produced better AI literacy outcomes than hybrid reflection-plus-rules approaches</li>
          <li>Reflection practices demonstrably failed to reduce AI-related harms in measurable ways</li>
          <li>The user-custodian model becomes technically impossible as AI systems become more agentic</li>
          <li>Critical thinking interventions show no effect on AI reliance behavior over time</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

